## What remains undone

The owner brake promises a revert for either a hold label on the merged PR or an owner challenge on its head version placed within the brake window (AGENTS.md:20-24; docs/V3.md section 14). PR #528 repairs delayed enforcement for signed challenges only. It does not preserve the other authorized veto channel.

At M, warrant/gate.py:1510-1511 skips an ended brake unless veto_challenge finds an owner challenge in the store. It never reads the PR's label. timely_veto at lines 1276-1285 likewise considers only signed challenges and expressly does not read label timing. Thus even a hold already observed by the gate before expiry is forgotten after a transient failure of the revert API. The label remains present, the exact revert remains available, but no further API call is made and the vetoed constitutional change remains in force.

The reproducer uses an actual fixture Git repository with a braked merge changing AGENTS.md, the production Base, store reader, enforce_brakes and open_revert. Only GitHub API responses and the two run times are simulated. There is no challenge record: the owner uses the promised hold-label channel. At until-1 the gate observes hold and attempts the exact revert, failing on POST git/trees. At until+1 the API is healthy and hold remains, but the gate makes no call. A healthy until-1 control opens the revert. This is a concrete lost retry, not an assumption about when an unobserved label appeared.

Expected: a proven timely hold survives delayed execution and failed attempts until its revert succeeds (with appropriate handling of label removal and genuinely late labels). Preserve or recover timely veto evidence; the regression must cover labels as well as signed challenges. No source changes or fix are filed in this verdict worktree.

